WEBVTT

00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:11.221
Many of the decisions that shape people's working days are small
ones: a meeting time, a notice, a change to a form.

00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:16.621
Because they are routine, they rarely get a second look, and that is
where barriers slip through.

00:00:16.743 --> 00:00:22.346
The Equity Pause for a routine decision gives you a short, honest
check before a decision goes out.

00:00:22.468 --> 00:00:24.279
Let's work through one.

00:00:24.401 --> 00:00:31.451
Imagine you supervise a team in the Disability Services Division,
and you plan to move your biweekly team meeting from Tuesday

00:00:31.451 --> 00:00:34.212
afternoon to Monday at eight in the morning.

00:00:34.779 --> 00:00:38.368
The page lays out Six steps to work through in order.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:45.628
The first, Consider the first question, invites you to browse
published questions for a decision like yours, then describe your

00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:51.323
own decision, identify the people affected, and note what evidence
or fuller review it may need.

00:00:51.446 --> 00:00:56.101
The second step, Consider community context, is marked If relevant.

00:00:56.223 --> 00:01:04.061
Your meeting is internal, so you might set it aside today, but when
a decision reaches particular Minnesota communities, those briefs

00:01:04.061 --> 00:01:07.012
describe access needs and what not to assume.

00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:14.830
The third step, Choose practical resources, points you to the
plain-language and language access checklists for anything people

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:15.701
will read.

00:01:15.823 --> 00:01:20.834
Your invitation is short, but its purpose, time and way to join
still need to be clear.

00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:24.232
Then comes the heart of the practice, Write your pause.

00:01:24.354 --> 00:01:31.017
Download the checklist and write your answers in a tool you normally
use, because this page does not save them.

00:01:31.583 --> 00:01:36.674
Begin with The decision, in one sentence: moving the team meeting to
Monday at eight.

00:01:36.797 --> 00:01:40.563
Next comes Who this affects, and who it affects differently.

00:01:40.686 --> 00:01:47.713
The help text asks you to think about language, disability,
schedule, location, technology, trust, and who might miss it

00:01:47.713 --> 00:01:48.540
entirely.

00:01:48.662 --> 00:01:55.838
Thinking it through, you realize that two team members start later
on Mondays because of caregiving, one person works a schedule with

00:01:55.838 --> 00:02:01.318
Mondays off, and a colleague who is Deaf relies on an interpreter
who must be booked well ahead.

00:02:01.884 --> 00:02:09.434
For Who can change this decision, and who you checked with, you note
that you set the meeting time as supervisor, and that you asked the

00:02:09.434 --> 00:02:11.067
team before settling it.

00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:19.028
Under Access needs you checked, the guidance names plain language,
an accessible format, interpretation or translation, timing, and a

00:02:19.028 --> 00:02:22.982
way to take part or respond that does not require one channel.

00:02:23.104 --> 00:02:30.655
So you confirm an interpreter for the new time, keep captions on,
and plan to send the agenda a day ahead so people can add items in

00:02:30.655 --> 00:02:31.412
writing.

00:02:31.979 --> 00:02:35.746
Then comes What you know, and what you do not know yet.

00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:40.079
You know the Tuesday slot now conflicts with another standing
meeting.

00:02:40.201 --> 00:02:47.523
You do not know whether an early Monday start works for everyone,
and that is the question you would ask with five more minutes.

00:02:47.646 --> 00:02:54.244
For One alternative that would reduce a barrier, you consider Monday
at ten instead of eight, choose it, and note why.

00:02:54.367 --> 00:03:02.018
Under What you chose not to assume, you write that silence after an
invitation does not mean the time works for everyone.

00:03:02.584 --> 00:03:06.544
The last two questions turn your thinking into follow-through.

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:13.558
For Your next step, and who is responsible, you will send the
revised invitation and book the interpreter yourself.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.522
For When you will look at this again, you choose a date about a
month out, after the new time has had an effect.

00:03:20.644 --> 00:03:23.518
Now move to Review your pause.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:31.102
Confirm the decision is specific, you checked access, you know who
can change it, you noted what you did not assume, and you have a

00:03:31.102 --> 00:03:32.656
next step with a date.

00:03:32.778 --> 00:03:38.856
If the reach or the stakes are larger than you first thought, move
to the Equity Analysis Toolkit.

00:03:39.422 --> 00:03:42.656
The page is candid about what makes a pause real.

00:03:42.778 --> 00:03:50.680
A pause completed after the decision already went out, or one that
says no one is affected when no one was asked, still needs work.

00:03:50.802 --> 00:03:58.564
A useful pause names a specific decision, the people it affects
differently, and one thing you changed or kept with a reason.

00:03:58.687 --> 00:04:04.409
If a question remains, the optional last step, Talk it through,
helps you find the right person.

00:04:04.531 --> 00:04:08.120
Nothing you read or download here goes to your supervisor.

00:04:08.242 --> 00:04:11.912
Pick one routine decision this week and give it ten minutes.
